#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# grad

#% text
# `grad(f, [x1, ..., xn])` gives the gradient `[d(f, x1), ..., d(f, xn)]` of the scalar `f`.

#% text
# `grad([f1, ..., fm], [x1, ..., xn])` gives the Jacobian matrix of the functions.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The variables are Cartesian coordinates.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
grad(x^2 + y^2, [x, y])
#% output
#= [2*x, 2*y]

#% cell
grad(x*y*z, [x, y, z])
#% output
#= [y*z, x*z, x*y]

#% cell
grad([x*y, x + y], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[y, x], [1, 1]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The gradient at a point points uphill; at a minimum it is zero:

#% cell
f = (x - 1)^2 + (y + 2)^2; g = grad(f, [x, y]); solve([g[1] == 0, g[2] == 0], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[x -> 1, y -> -2]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The curl of a gradient is zero:

#% cell
curl(grad(x^2*y*z, [x, y, z]), [x, y, z])
#% output
#= [0, 0, 0]

#% text
# The divergence of the gradient is the Laplacian:

#% cell
[div(grad(x^3*y, [x, y]), [x, y]), laplacian(x^3*y, [x, y])]
#% output
#= [6*x*y, 6*x*y]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `div`, `curl`, `laplacian`, `d`
